From: pmetzger@shearson.com (Perry E. Metzger)
Subject: PPL: Intellectual property protection

> From: Eli Brandt <ebrandt@jarthur.claremont.edu>
> 
> > Copyrights one can imagine arising out of contracts associated with
> > the purchase of a book -- i.e. if you want to buy my book, you have to
> > agree not to copy it.
> 
> Could you expand upon this?  I can require that you do all sorts of
> things in order to buy my book, but these requirements are not
> generally extended to books acquired without such contracts.
> Copyright, on the other hand, applies to precisely this case -- when
> you do have a contract, you don't need copyright law.

I was merely indicating that one could imagine property rights to one's
texts and programs arising naturally out of contract law without the
necessity for government fiat, but one could not similarly imagine patent
law per se (although trade secret agreements might still apply). My use
of the term "copyright" shouldn't indicate that I believe in copyright
qua copyright.

Perry

