Date: Tue, 26 Jan 93 11:04:04 EST
From: pmetzger@shearson.com (Perry E. Metzger)
Subject: EVOL: Stupidity and natural selection

> From: Robin Hanson <hanson@ptolemy.arc.nasa.gov>
> 
> Perry E. Metzger writes:
> >Well, you'd expect that half the trades made on the exchange must by
> >definition be money losing. After all, futures are a zero sum game, unlike,
> >say, the equities market. 
> 
> I think I disagree.  Can you offer a more precise definition of
> "zero-sum" so that we could evaluate your claim?  Both futures and
> equities offer a net return on one's investment, returns competitive
> with each other when corrected for market-correlated risk.  

Equities are shares in companies. If the company gets more valuable, it
is possible for an investor to get richer without anyone else getting
poorer. On the other hand, the futures market is a betting market in which
whenever I make money it is because someone else has lost it. Its like
a casino. This is not to say that I dislike the futures market -- it provides
valuable insurance services. This is not to say, however, that insurance
is not a zero sum game.

Perry

