Date: Tue, 26 Jan 93 11:26:08 EST
From: pmetzger@shearson.com (Perry E. Metzger)
Subject: Market Cornering

Thought I'd explain what market corners mean, since people here don't seem
to understand the concept.

> From: Pamela Williard and Mark Pickens <autarch@well.sf.ca.us>
>  
> > It *has* occurred.  My great-grandfather retired at 26 by cornering
> > the dried apple market.  Many other people have made a lot of money
> > by "market manipulations".
>  
> Dale, are you seriously telling us that your great-grandfather purchased all
> the dried apples available (or all the stuff necessary to make dried
> apples), pushed the price artificially high, pulling in enough unearned
> money to cover those purchases, with enough left over to retire?  
>  
> The fact is, your ancestor made a lot of money selling dried apples.
> Period.  He had no more ability to "corner the market" than the Hunt
> brothers and for exactly the same reason.  (They don't call them "laws" of
> economics for nothing.)

I must admit to finding the notion that Dale's grandfather managed to aquire
rights to all the apples in the world, or even in North America, in such
a way as to actually control that market completely, to be rather difficult
to swallow.

Even were he able to do this, the notion that he had "cornered" the market
also makes no sense, as there is not now nor has there ever been a liquid
market in dried apple contracts that would have permitted him to achieve
a corner. A corner, for those of you who do not know, is when someone buys
more of a liquid fungible contract on an exchange than exists. You can do
this because some people will sell short, thus permitting more of the fungible
to be in circulation than there really is. If you aquire absolutely all
the contracts you can, you suddenly find yourself in the interesting position
that all the short sellers, in order to cover themselves, have to pay you
an arbitrarily large amount of money to give back the fungible that they
borrowed, likely from you! The old wall street rhyme is "He who sells what
isn't his'n // Buys it back or goes to prison."

This used to occur once in a great while on some exchanges, but modern
exchange rules usually prevent it from happening. Certainly it has happened
more often by accident than by intention (such as when the House of Morgan
and the Rockefellers got themselves into a bidding war over a pacific
northwest railway in 1907 -- but thats another story). There are many
great stories about various people who've tried to corner markets and
have failed, because if you don't actually manage to aquire a corner
you will likely lose lots of money because you've driven up the price in
the act of trying to buy every contract you can. (Jay Gould nearly lost
his skin this way -- the Hunt Brothers were not so lucky when they tried
to corner the silver market.)

Anyway, for Dale's great grandpa to have cornered the market in dried apples,
we would have to assume that the level of sophistication in dried apple
speculation was sufficient that he could aquire the whole supply available
and that substantial short selling would occur in the apple and dried
apple commodities markets. Such markets have never, to my knowledge, been
sufficiently sophisticated (i.e. possessing fungible contracts that get
sold short) to permit this.

Perry

